focussing only on the current feature set, these are some things that
would get my motor running:

* reduced size (binary, dungeon, etc.).  for example, there should only
    be about 40 dungeon levels max, something like:
    1 astral plane
    1 final challenge level (randomly choose one of the elementals)
    10 normal dungeon levels, each one interesting, in some way
        (i.e. a shop, behive, barracks, zoo, etc.)
    3 - 5 mines levels (1 mine level change = 3 normal level changes,
        so assuming the mines entrance is on main dungeon level 4 and
        the mine has 4 levels the mines exit would reconnect to the
        main dungeon at level 19!).
    3 - 5 quest levels (and quest goal should be randomized somehow).
    1 castle
    1 big room
    1 ft. ludious
    10 maze/gehennom levels (not every game will have Asmodeus, Orcus,
        Baazelbub, Vlad (should be a single normal maze level), Dead
        Valley and so on).
    1 wizard level
    1 sanctuary
--------
    34 - 38 total

* eliminate need for spoilers!
    assume hackers are morons (i.e. they don't know AD&D or Bell, Book,
        Candle literature etc.).
    make fountains/thrones/sinks, etc. show their diverse effects much
        more frequently.
    provide more clues and give better feedback (i.e. the priesthood
        interactions are currently completely non-intuitive).
    and so on.

* consistent, coherent, user-friendly interface in all areas:
    eliminate put on vs wear nit picking, and so on.
    allow easier access to containers and moving items.
    make smart assumptions (i.e. carrying a key means auto-door
        or auto-container opening under the right conditions,
        continuing to eat/read/whatever should check for the
        previous item first before going into the general interface,
        and so on, there's many more).
    either eliminate alignment or do it right.  what's with the
        mysterious forces during level changes in gehennom???
    eliminate AD&D stats and scales (let Str, Dex, etc. range from
        0 to 100, experience levels are irrelevant after a certain
        point, and so on.).
    which orc is hitting me with a curved sword as opposed to an
        orcish bow?

* more emphasis on magic-user vs. hack-n-slasher strategy differences.
    spells and spellbooks need to be completely re-worked.  weapon usage
    rules need tweaking.

* more unique role/character traits and differences (i.e. constrain
        quest items, expanded abilities - rogues should be able to
        steal, healers should always know how to heal themselves,
        wizards somehow identify magic, and so on).

i have yet to see a usage of a magic portal that wouldn't be better
implemented as an up/down stairwell ...

